Serve this repo as plain static files. Do not run Jekyll.

WHY (2026-08-26). GitHub Pages runs Jekyll over the whole repo unless this file exists.
Nothing here needs it — index.html is a single static page with no front matter, so Jekyll
was only ever copying files while adding a build step that can queue, throttle or fail.

Two things it was actually costing us:

  1. BUILD TIME AND FRAGILITY. Every push made Pages walk 467 tracked files through Jekyll
     before publishing. With several sessions shipping in the same hour that is a lot of
     avoidable work in front of the thing we care about, which is one HTML file.

  2. UNDERSCORE FILES WERE SILENTLY UNPUBLISHED. Jekyll ignores anything starting with "_",
     so _edgelog_live.html returned 404 on the live site while sitting happily in the repo.
     Nothing links to it today, but the next file named that way would vanish with no error
     anywhere.

Note what this does NOT fix: GitHub's soft limit of ~10 Pages builds per hour on a
branch-sourced site. On 2026-08-26 main took 15 pushes inside the 07:00 hour and the site
stopped updating at v73.284 while main was on v73.287. `tools/wt.py ship` now counts recent
pushes and warns before you cross that line. The permanent fix is to move Pages onto a
GitHub Actions source; that is a repository setting, not something in this tree.
